            <p>Athens International processed more than 28 million travelers in recent years, with North American traffic representing one of the fastest-growing origin markets. The airport's dual-terminal layout - a Main Terminal and a Satellite linked by an underground passage - efficiently channels arriving passengers toward domestic connections that scatter them across the archipelago.</p>
            <h2>Airlines Flying from New York to Greece</h2>
            <p>A crowded field of carriers competes for share on this lucrative corridor, with seasonal nonstop operations battling against connecting itineraries through major European hubs.</p>
            <h3>Full-Service Carriers</h3>
            <h4>Delta Air Lines</h4>
            <p>Delta dominates the nonstop landscape from New York to Athens, leveraging its JFK hub and SkyTeam partnerships.</p>
            <p><b>Distinguishing characteristics:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Daily nonstop service from JFK to Athens during peak season (May through October), typically launching around 5:55 PM and touching down 1at 0:20 AM the following morning</li>
                <li>Boeing 767-400ER and Airbus A330-900neo equipment on the transatlantic leg</li>
                <li>SkyMiles accumulation with broad partner redemption possibilities</li>
                <li>Delta One suites featuring lie-flat seating and Mediterranean-influenced dining</li>
                <li>Robust domestic feeder network from LGA and regional airports into JFK</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>United Airlines</h4>
            <p>United exploits its Newark fortress hub and Star Alliance relationships to capture northern New Jersey and Manhattan's west side traffic.</p>
            <p><b>Notable attributes:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Seasonal nonstop service from Newark (EWR) to Athens, generally operating with evening departures</li>
                <li>Boeing 787-10 Dreamliner deployment, offering improved cabin pressure and humidity</li>
                <li>MileagePlus earning with extensive Star Alliance redemption options</li>
                <li>Polaris business class with lie-flat pods and multi-course dining</li>
                <li>Strong Connecticut and northern New Jersey market penetration</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Emirates</h4>
            <p>The Dubai-based luxury carrier offers an alternative routing that, while longer, delivers exceptional service standards.</p>
            <p><b>Competitive positioning:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>JFK to Athens via Dubai with competitive all-in pricing</li>
                <li>A380 and Boeing 777-300ER equipment on the transatlantic sectors</li>
                <li>ICE entertainment system with thousands of channels</li>
                <li>Skywards loyalty program with generous accrual rates</li>
                <li>Dubai stopover possibilities at minimal incremental cost</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>American Airlines</h4>
            <p>Americans' presence fluctuates seasonally, with direct New York-Athens service appearing and disappearing based on demand forecasts.</p>
            <p><b>Key features:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Seasonal JFK-Athens nonstop during peak summer months</li>
                <li>Connections through Philadelphia (PHL) with well-timed onward segments</li>
                <li>AAdvantage program earning with British Airways and Iberia reciprocity</li>
                <li>Flagship Business class on transatlantic segments</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>British Airways</h4>
            <p>The British flag carrier funnels New York traffic through its London Heathrow hub.</p>
            <p><b>Appeal:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Multiple daily frequencies from both JFK and Newark to London</li>
                <li>Convenient for travelers combining Greece with UK exploration</li>
                <li>Executive Club Avios earning and redemption</li>
                <li>Club World and First Class products</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Lufthansa / Austrian / Swiss</h4>
            <p>Star Alliance connections through Frankfurt, Munich, Vienna, or Zurich.</p>
            <p><b>Value proposition:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Reliable European connections with manageable layover durations</li>
                <li>Consistent premium cabin standards</li>
                <li>MileagePlus and Miles &amp; More earning opportunities</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Air France / KLM</h4>
            <p>SkyTeam alliance routing through Paris or Amsterdam.</p>
            <p><b>Benefits:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Strong European connectivity</li>
                <li>Flying Blue and SkyMiles dual earning possibilities</li>
                <li>Useful for combining Greece with Western European destinations</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Turkish Airlines</h4>
            <p>Istanbul-based carrier frequently undercuts European rivals on price.</p>
            <p><b>Advantages:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Often, the lowest total fare through strategic Istanbul connections</li>
                <li>Globally acclaimed onboard cuisine and hospitality</li>
                <li>Extensive network into Greek islands and secondary cities</li>
                <li>Generous complimentary baggage allowance</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Low-Cost and Hybrid Options</h3>
            <h4>PLAY Airlines</h4>
            <p>Icelandic budget carrier with connections through Reykjavik.</p>
            <p><b>Profile:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Attractive base fares from JFK</li>
                <li>à la carte service model</li>
                <li>Primarily attracts price-sensitive leisure travelers with a flexible schedule.</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Norse Atlantic Airways</h4>
            <p>Norwegian long-haul low-cost operator.</p>
            <p><b>Positioning:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Ultra-low introductory fares</li>
                <li>Pay-for-what-you-need architecture</li>
                <li>Limited schedule but significant savings potential for flexible travelers</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Regional Greek Operators</h3>
            <h4>Aegean Airlines</h4>
            <p>Greece's largest carrier and Star Alliance anchor, handling the critical Athens-to-island distribution.</p>
            <p><b>Function:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Domestic connections from Athens to all significant island destinations</li>
                <li>Codeshare arrangements with Lufthansa and United for seamless through ticketing</li>
                <li>Excellent on-time performance and regional service standards</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Olympic Air</h4>
            <p>Aegean's subsidiary, serving smaller islands and secondary mainland points.</p>
            <h4>Codeshare Ecosystems</h4>
            <p>Invisible partnerships multiply apparent options:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Delta and Air France/KLM/Alitalia coordinate through SkyTeam</li>
                <li>United and Lufthansa/Austrian/Swiss/Brussels Airlines align through Star Alliance</li>
                <li>American and British Airways/Iberia/Finnair connect via oneworld</li>
                <li>These arrangements protect travelers during irregular operations and streamline mileage accrual</li>
            </ul>
            <h2>Seasonal Variations</h2>
            <p>Ticket prices and seat availability swing dramatically across the calendar, driven by Mediterranean weather patterns, American vacation schedules, and the Orthodox liturgical calendar.</p>
            <h3>Peak Season: June Through August</h3>
            <p>Summer transforms Greece into the planet's most sought-after Mediterranean destination.</p>
            <h4>Market dynamics:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Economy round-trips escalate to $1,000-$2,200</li>
                <li>Business class climbs to $3,500-$6,500</li>
                <li>Nonstop frequencies peak from both JFK and Newark</li>
                <li>Three- to four-month advance purchase essential for reasonable fares</li>
                <li>Island accommodations and ferry berths are approaching sellout</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Premium pricing windows:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><B>Mid-July through August - </B>Absolute apex; Athenians evacuate to islands; international tourists flood in</li>
                <li><B>US Independence Day week -</B> American demand spike</li>
                <li><B>August 15 (Dormition of the Virgin) </B>- Major Orthodox feast day triggering domestic travel compression</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Shoulder Windows: April to May, September to October</h3>
            <p>These transitional months reward adaptable travelers with superb value and agreeable conditions.</p>
            <h4>Why shoulders excel:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Fares retreat 25-40% from summer highs</li>
                <li>Temperatures are ideal for archaeological trekking and coastal hiking</li>
                <li>Crowds dissipate at the Acropolis, Delphi, and Epidaurus</li>
                <li>Aegean waters retain swimmable warmth through October</li>
                <li>Spring wildflowers carpet the mainland hillsides</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Low Season: November Through March</h3>
            <p>Winter unlocks budget possibilities alongside authentic local immersion.</p>
            <h4>Financial advantages:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Economy tickets frequently surface at $500-$850 round-trip</li>
                <li>Business class costs $2,000-$3,500</li>
                <li>Athens reveals its genuine urban character, stripped of tourist artifice</li>
                <li>Top-tier restaurants accept walk-ins without reservations</li>
                <li>Hotel rates collapse, particularly on islands</li>
            </ul>
            <h4>Seasonal trade-offs:</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>Numerous island hotels and taverns shutter for the winter</li>
                <li>Ferry timetables thin dramatically; some islands become unreachable</li>
                <li>Rainfall increases; mountain regions may see snow accumulation</li>
                <li>Daylight hours contract, constraining sightseeing</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Calendar Events Distorting Costs:</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><B>Orthodox Easter (variable March-May date) - </B>Greece's paramount religious observance; prices explode; reservations require a year-ahead commitment</li>
                <li><B>Athens Epidaurus Festival (June-August) - </B>Ancient theater performances drawing culture tourists</li>
                <li><B>Christmas and New Year - </B>Moderate Athens activity; islands largely dormant</li>
                <li><B>Carnival (Apokries, February/March) -</B> Regional festivities with modest tourism impact</li>
            </ul>
