            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: Are there direct flights from New York to Greece?</h3>
            <p>Yes, Delta operates seasonal nonstop service from JFK to Athens during peak months, typically May through October. United offers seasonal nonstop flights from Newark. Winter travel requires connections through European or Middle Eastern hubs.</p>

            <h3>Q2: What is the cheapest time to fly from New York to Greece?</h3>
            <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, with economy round-trips often available at $500-$850. For summer travel, book 60-90 days ahead and consider shoulder months like May or September for optimal value and weather balance.</p>

            <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit Greece from the US?</h3>
            <p>No visa is required for tourism stays under 90 days within any 180-day Schengen period. A valid passport with at least three months' validity beyond departure suffices-no additional documentation needed for standard visits.</p>

            <h3>Q4: Which New York airport is best for Greece flights?</h3>
            <p>JFK offers the most nonstop options during peak season, particularly Delta's daily service. Newark provides United's seasonal nonstop and strong Star Alliance connections. Both are accessible from Manhattan; choose based on your airline preference and origin location.</p>

            <h3>Q5: How do I get from Athens airport to the city center or the islands?</h3>
            <p>Metro Line 3 reaches central Athens in 40 minutes for €9. Airport Express Bus X95 serves Syntagma Square. Taxis charge €40 fixed daytime rate. For islands, transfer to Piraeus Port by Metro, bus, or taxi for ferry connections.</p>

            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Greece?</h3>
            <p>The Euro is Greece's currency. US dollars are not accepted. Credit cards are widely accepted at hotels, restaurants, and shops. ATMs are plentiful throughout Athens and the major islands. Notify your bank before traveling to prevent card blocks.</p>

            <h3>Q7: What should I pack for a Greek island vacation?</h3>
            <p>Light breathable clothing, comfortable walking shoes for archaeological sites, sturdy sandals, reef-safe sunscreen, a wide-brimmed hat, and a light jacket for evening breezes. Modest dress required for monastery visits. Power adapter for European outlets.</p>

            <h3>Q8: Is Greece safe for American tourists?</h3>
            <p>Greece is among Europe's safest destinations. Standard precautions apply regarding pickpockets in tourist areas and on the Athens Metro. Violent crime against tourists is rare. Police presence is visible and helpful. The emergency number is 112 throughout Europe.</p>

            <h3>Q9: How do I travel between Greek islands?</h3>
            <p>Ferries operated by Blue Star, Hellenic Seaways, Sea Jets, and Minoan Lines connect major islands. Domestic flights by Aegean and Sky Express serve larger islands. Book ferries well in advance during July and August. Island-hopping passes available for extensive itineraries.</p>

            <h3>Q10: What is the best way to avoid jet lag on New York to Greece flights?</h3>
            <p>Choose evening departures arriving in Athens in the morning. Sleep on the flight using eye masks and earplugs. Upon arrival, expose yourself to daylight immediately, stay awake until local evening, and avoid heavy meals. Hydration throughout the journey is essential.</p>














